#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537999} :activity/id #uuid "9c2633a4-2f33-5904-a37a-770c0e5abd32" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048985 :topic/id #uuid "d10de7de-f23e-5cf3-b20d-fb6542a1ea1d" :topic/math-academy-id 1218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8a7cfdf-560a-5dda-bb00-88b7b7db33a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098512} {:answer/id #uuid "19ba7c3f-bf30-5918-99d4-41a94c11d7c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098513} {:answer/id #uuid "21b956ae-a328-5df2-9861-617d0c217f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098514} {:answer/id #uuid "29843d37-166e-57c1-a30b-fcf65e24db6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098515} {:answer/id #uuid "7107455b-60e8-57ee-ae33-c39125e56685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186098514} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ac3cf8d-6c84-5024-abb1-2a750c1f027c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "be45f13d-84b8-5eb7-9480-670af18ba1fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-15331" :question/problem "A cinema hall can hold $120$ people. The price of each ticket is $$10.$ The cost of maintaining the cinema is made up of a fixed cost of $$400$ plus a cost of $$0.25$ per person. How many tickets should the cinema sell to maximize its profit?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ tickets, the cinema has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=10\\cdot x=10x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{400}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{0.25x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =10x-(400+0.25x) \\\\  & =9.75x-400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 120.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=120$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<120.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=9.75.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nOur candidates are $x=0$ and $x=120.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =9.75(0)-400=-400 \\\\ P(120) & =9.75(120)-400=770\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the cinema will have a maximum profit level when it sells $120$ tickets."} :step/id #uuid "1e9fa964-7041-5ffc-83b1-6b0438a925d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141d1a25-fba8-5358-bdf7-47d4083d0230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$243\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537267} {:answer/id #uuid "4e64df2e-399d-5b8f-a2f3-3cf2f8c50241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$357\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537391} {:answer/id #uuid "6758ee03-03a0-5b60-bde7-55ba18790097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$270\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537432} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3fc12f-fcff-5a8f-956b-4326f0c686ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$292\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537440} {:answer/id #uuid "9619c210-60c8-533d-bf4f-57ac601c6744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$412\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537432} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe295f2-3c78-53d7-a4bc-8cad7afb171c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4cb999cb-e9bf-5cd0-a21c-a3857e783014" :question/math-academy-id "q-87593" :question/problem "A factory spends $C(x)=100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousand dollars per month to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$100\\,000$ per unit. Given that the factory can produce between $1$ and $10$ items per month, what is the maximum monthly profit it can make?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=100\\cdot x=100x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =100x-(100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+147x-100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 10.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-42x+147,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-42x+147 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-14x+49 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check if $x=7$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=6x-42.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(7)=6(7)-42=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm or deny that $x=7$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ Therefore, we should consider $x=7$ as one of the candidates.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=7$ and $x=10.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =(1)^{3}-21(1)^{2}+147(1)-100=27 \\\\ P(7) & =(7)^{3}-21(7)^{2}+147(7)-100=243 \\\\ P(10) & =(10)^{3}-21(10)^{2}+147(10)-100=270\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $270\\cdot $1\\,000=$270\\,000.$"} :step/id #uuid "4dd5af7f-a91c-54b8-878b-4a18da96bc9b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538052}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16494579-7656-5c2d-a785-de9c67da7d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "188" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537274} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb55bb9f-d933-52e4-9689-fefd9cbfbbcf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7f361b6d-88da-56f4-bb4f-1191d0218d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-347278" :question/problem "A tutoring center offers private lesson packages. When the price of a package is $$40,$ the center books all $80$ available sessions. For every $$4$ increase in the package price, one fewer session is booked. If the cost of providing each session is $$16,$ what price should the center charge to maximize its profit?\n\nTo maximize profit, the center should charge $$$\n{{field-1}}\nper session." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of unbooked sessions. Then the number of **booked** sessions is $(80-x),$ and the **price per session** is $(40+4x).$\n\nSo, the total revenue is\n\n$$\nR(x)=(80-x)(40+4x).\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\nR(x)=-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200.\n$$\n\nThe cost of providing each booked session is $$16,$ so the total cost is\n\n$$\nC(x)=16(80-x)=1280-16x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200)-(1280-16x) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{2}+296x+1920.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given the domain $0\\le x\\le 80.$\n\nTo find the maximum, we take the derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-8x+296.\n$$\n\nSolving ${P}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\nx=37.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(x)=-8<0,$ this point is a maximum. Since $P(x)$ is quadratic, this maximum is global.\n\nNow, if $x=37$ sessions are unbooked, then the price is:\n\n$$\n40+4(37)=188.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize profit, the center should charge $$\\,\\,188$ per session."} :step/id #uuid "6df0f32d-2973-5214-b35a-1fd2363c1870"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d62f0bd-935b-5b00-a2c0-88c9776e3a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537291} {:answer/id #uuid "5069e6da-e9ae-553f-8f4a-25dc832f7acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537397} {:answer/id #uuid "7325bf9e-6b3c-590c-8efe-c6c988414b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537456} {:answer/id #uuid "b9910e6d-7274-5ad8-9582-87f427993b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537590} {:answer/id #uuid "bfddd2cc-fb5b-506f-910b-2fc1d8be0dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537291} :answer-field/id #uuid "465ed207-d844-5919-a8df-b87293b0bcf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "da64bda4-339a-50c3-828b-c79ac0fe60b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-87576" :question/problem "A daycare center can take care of between $10$ and $20$ children. The fee that parents must pay each month depends on the number of children enrolled in the center. If there are $10$ children enrolled, parents must pay $$40.$ On the other hand, if there are more than $10$ children enrolled, the price drops by $$2$ for each child. All the parents pay the same monthly fee.\n\nHow many children must enroll for the center to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $10+x$ children enrolled, then the monthly fee is $40-2x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(10+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of children enrolled}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{(40-2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{monthly fee}} \\\\  & =(10+x)(40-2x) \\\\  & =400-20x+40x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =400+20x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the center can only take care between $10$ and $20$ children, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & \\le x+10\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=20-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =20 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the interval $0<x<10.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the center must have $10+5=15$ children enrolled to maximize its revenue."} :step/id #uuid "b36a3f97-5787-5772-8e16-fde45721c2ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "989ed443-1a83-563a-80ca-7360914297e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$400$ units per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537519} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c2e2ef-4e7c-53d2-a226-3d5810d7e74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$200$ units per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537619} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f9e17b-f328-54e4-ab9d-74a99d605704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$40$ units per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537639} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e0e802-95ad-550c-9901-8b89631a6f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$500$ units per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537705} {:answer/id #uuid "f906ef92-e5c6-577b-a75e-78d163c0e750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$250$ units per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537519} :answer-field/id #uuid "68dc796d-0c9f-52c2-87ac-381b9f5b4a1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3238715a-ab0d-5fbe-809b-2aa2ec76dbe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-119056" :question/problem "A company spends $C(x)=0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $$12$ per unit. Given that the company can produce at most $500$ units per month, how many units should the company produce per month to maximize its monthly profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the company has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=12\\cdot x=12x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =12x-(0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680) \\\\  & =8x-0.01{x}^{2}-680.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 500.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<500.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=8-0.02x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 8-0.02x & =0 \\\\ x & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=400$ lies in the interval $0<x<500.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=400$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.02<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=400$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=400$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the company will have a maximum monthly profit when it produces $400$ units per month."} :step/id #uuid "c2ce17c2-aa7c-5e08-a132-c77e2f0ad3b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538028}}] :activity/title "Optimization Problems in Economics" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537215}]}
